-- title:
--   Convexity of a cone of half-aperture at most a right angle
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ be a vector, and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ satisfy
--   $$0 \le \vartheta \le \frac{\pi}{2}.$$
--   Write $\tilde V(v,\vartheta)$ for the one-sided cone with apex at the origin, axis direction $v$ and half-aperture $\vartheta$, defined exactly as in the formalization (`cone v ϑ`) by
--   $$\tilde V(v,\vartheta) \;=\; \Bigl\{\, h \in E \;:\; h \ne 0 \ \text{ and } \ \cos\vartheta < \frac{\langle v, h\rangle}{\lVert h\rVert} \,\Bigr\};$$
--   for a unit axis $v$ and $0 \le \vartheta \le \pi$ this is precisely the set of nonzero vectors whose angle with $v$ is strictly smaller than $\vartheta$. Then
--   $$\tilde V(v,\vartheta) \text{ is a convex subset of } E,$$
--   i.e. for all $p, q \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta)$ and all $s, t \ge 0$ with $s + t = 1$ one has $s p + t q \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta)$.
--
--   The upper bound $\vartheta \le \pi/2$ is what makes the statement true: a one-sided cone opening by more than a right angle need not be convex, since it can contain two vectors whose segment passes on the far side of the apex. The lower bound $0 \le \vartheta$ is a genuine hypothesis as well: the defining inequality sees $\vartheta$ only through $\cos\vartheta$, so $\tilde V(v,-\vartheta) = \tilde V(v,\vartheta)$, and a negative aperture therefore does not make the cone empty but reproduces the cone of aperture $\lvert\vartheta\rvert$ — which for $\lvert\vartheta\rvert > \pi/2$ is not convex. Convexity is the basic structural property one appeals to whenever a segment joining two points of a cone must be known to stay inside it.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis normalizes $v$; the axis is an arbitrary vector of $E$, and the statement is proved in an arbitrary real inner product space rather than in $\mathbb{R}^d$. Since $v$ is not assumed to be a unit vector, the membership condition is the displayed inequality on $\langle v,h\rangle/\lVert h\rVert$ rather than a condition on the angle; the degenerate axis $v = 0$ is covered as well, and there the cone is empty, since $\cos\vartheta \ge 0$ throughout the admitted range.
